{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-34327" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $A={⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 0}$\n- $B={⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x=2y}$\n- $C={⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x-y=0}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070328/state.json" "file_sha256" "ebc34a0e443fc8b6185960f05b82221ae90834d3be28472a5cecfe8cf47131e3"} {"question" "q-34327" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is a subspace. It actually represents the whole space ${R}^{2}$, since the coordinates of any vector $⟨x,y⟩$ satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 0.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x=2y$ since $0=2\\cdot 0.$ So, $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. It also represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x-y=0$ since $0-0=0.$ So, $0$ lies in $C.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070328/state.json" "file_sha256" "ebc34a0e443fc8b6185960f05b82221ae90834d3be28472a5cecfe8cf47131e3"} {"question" "q-34327" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070328/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "83d9b36f9495424f85d1bd2713a5e9f340f57d3f9bf6236fd42948ed49985960"} {"question" "q-34327" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070328/state.json" "file_sha256" "ebc34a0e443fc8b6185960f05b82221ae90834d3be28472a5cecfe8cf47131e3"} {"question" "q-34327" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070328/state.json" "file_sha256" "ebc34a0e443fc8b6185960f05b82221ae90834d3be28472a5cecfe8cf47131e3"} {"question" "q-34327" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070328/state.json" "file_sha256" "ebc34a0e443fc8b6185960f05b82221ae90834d3be28472a5cecfe8cf47131e3"} {"question" "q-35595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070328/assets/df3b62554600601401d14d3b6758a3c350cf8cddc3788e79a3ba456a5a19c60a.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070328/state.json" "file_sha256" "ebc34a0e443fc8b6185960f05b82221ae90834d3be28472a5cecfe8cf47131e3"} {"question" "q-35595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents the origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram III is not a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070328/state.json" "file_sha256" "ebc34a0e443fc8b6185960f05b82221ae90834d3be28472a5cecfe8cf47131e3"} {"question" "q-35595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070328/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "83d9b36f9495424f85d1bd2713a5e9f340f57d3f9bf6236fd42948ed49985960"} {"question" "q-35595" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070328/state.json" "file_sha256" "ebc34a0e443fc8b6185960f05b82221ae90834d3be28472a5cecfe8cf47131e3"} {"question" "q-35595" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070328/state.json" "file_sha256" "ebc34a0e443fc8b6185960f05b82221ae90834d3be28472a5cecfe8cf47131e3"} {"question" "q-35595" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070328/state.json" "file_sha256" "ebc34a0e443fc8b6185960f05b82221ae90834d3be28472a5cecfe8cf47131e3"} {"question" "q-74255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $A={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}<\\frac{1}{2}}$\n- $B={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:5x-y-z=0}$\n- $C={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:5-x-z=0}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070328/state.json" "file_sha256" "ebc34a0e443fc8b6185960f05b82221ae90834d3be28472a5cecfe8cf47131e3"} {"question" "q-74255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace. Indeed, the vector $u=⟨\\frac{1}{4},0,0⟩$ is in $A$, since\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4}<\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nbut, $4u=⟨1,0,0⟩$ is not in $B$, since \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}>\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $5x-y-z=0$ since $5\\cdot 0-0-0=0.$ So $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $5-x-z=0$ since $5-0-0\\ne 0.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070328/state.json" "file_sha256" "ebc34a0e443fc8b6185960f05b82221ae90834d3be28472a5cecfe8cf47131e3"} {"question" "q-74255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070328/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "83d9b36f9495424f85d1bd2713a5e9f340f57d3f9bf6236fd42948ed49985960"} {"question" "q-74255" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070328/state.json" "file_sha256" "ebc34a0e443fc8b6185960f05b82221ae90834d3be28472a5cecfe8cf47131e3"} {"question" "q-74255" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070328/state.json" "file_sha256" "ebc34a0e443fc8b6185960f05b82221ae90834d3be28472a5cecfe8cf47131e3"} {"question" "q-74255" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070328/state.json" "file_sha256" "ebc34a0e443fc8b6185960f05b82221ae90834d3be28472a5cecfe8cf47131e3"} {"question" "q-110761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $A={⟨2t,2+t⟩\\,:\\,t\\in R}$\n- $B={⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x-y=0}$\n- $C={⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:y=1}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070328/state.json" "file_sha256" "ebc34a0e443fc8b6185960f05b82221ae90834d3be28472a5cecfe8cf47131e3"} {"question" "q-110761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. If the origin belongs to this subset, we would have, for some $t$:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ 2+t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives us\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2t=0 \\\\ 2+t=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the system has no solution. So, $0$ is not in $A.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace.  It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x-y=0$ since $0-0=0.$ So, $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $y=1$ since $0\\ne 1.$ So, $0$ is not in $C.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070328/state.json" "file_sha256" "ebc34a0e443fc8b6185960f05b82221ae90834d3be28472a5cecfe8cf47131e3"} {"question" "q-110761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070328/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "83d9b36f9495424f85d1bd2713a5e9f340f57d3f9bf6236fd42948ed49985960"} {"question" "q-110761" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070328/state.json" "file_sha256" "ebc34a0e443fc8b6185960f05b82221ae90834d3be28472a5cecfe8cf47131e3"} {"question" "q-110761" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070328/state.json" "file_sha256" "ebc34a0e443fc8b6185960f05b82221ae90834d3be28472a5cecfe8cf47131e3"} {"question" "q-110761" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "c